WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.433 --> 00:00:02.433
[music]

2
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.066
[Instructor] We've got a "for() loop"
set up, so now when we press Play,

3
00:00:09.066 --> 00:00:12.100
you can see the 3 different
enemies spawning around.

4
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:18.233
If we're going to increase the number
of enemies that spawn in our game,

5
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.066
we'll actually need
to be able to pass information

6
00:00:21.066 --> 00:00:23.066
to our method using Parameters.

7
00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:25.733
In our SpawnManager Script,

8
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:28.200
we have our "SpawnEnemyWave()" method.

9
00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:31.300
Now, how do we actually
add Parameters to our method,

10
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:34.733
so that way we can tell
it numbers of enemies

11
00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:38.000
that we want to spawn over time?
In fact, it's fairly easy.

12
00:00:38.233 --> 00:00:40.500
What we can do is,
we can type in "int...

13
00:00:41.233 --> 00:00:44.733
enemiesToSpawn,"

14
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.133
and now this creates
an "enemiesToSpawn" variable

15
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.900
that we can then use
throughout our code.

16
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.800
So, instead of ending
our for() loop when "i < 3,"

17
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:58.566
we can end our for() loop
when "i < enemiesToSpawn."

18
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:02.800
We've been using
Parameters the entire time

19
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.166
with a lot of our methods,
to be able to pass in information

20
00:01:06.166 --> 00:01:09.500
like enemyPrefab,
or the rotation,

21
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.866
and in this case,
we can use something

22
00:01:13.266 --> 00:01:15.566
as simple as an integer variable

23
00:01:16.266 --> 00:01:19.100
to pass in the number of enemies
that we want to spawn,

24
00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.300
every time we call this method.

25
00:01:21.433 --> 00:01:24.300
One thing you'll notice
is our SpawnEnemyWave() method

26
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.433
at our Start() method is pretty angry
with us right now,

27
00:01:27.833 --> 00:01:29.900
because it actually needs
a number to start with.

28
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.933
So, we can press 3,

29
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:34.900
and now when we play our game, 
we should just see 3 enemies

30
00:01:34.966 --> 00:01:36.966
spawn once again.

31
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.366
You can see that adding Parameters

32
00:01:43.366 --> 00:01:45.733
to our methods can be
a fairly easy process.

33
00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:48.933
Then, if we wanted to, we could just
use something as simple as a comma,

34
00:01:49.366 --> 00:01:52.266
and state another random 
thing to be able to...

35
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:57.866
use a second variable. And, we can add
in as many Parameters as we want,

36
00:01:57.866 --> 00:02:01.266
but it's best to keep the Parameters
to as minimum as possible

37
00:02:01.266 --> 00:02:03.200
so that way you're not using
too many things,

38
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.000
if you don't need to.

39
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.400
One other point to mention here,
just to notice,

40
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.966
you can see
that here in our SpawnEnemyWave(),

41
00:02:10.166 --> 00:02:12.066
we have a "void" method,

42
00:02:12.266 --> 00:02:15.266
and then in our GenerateSpawnPosition(),
we have a "Vector3".

43
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:19.466
So, you can see here the difference 
in when you would really use

44
00:02:19.466 --> 00:02:22.066
a "void" method,
versus a method that returns something.

45
00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:26.566
Where with our GenerateSpawnPosition(),
we want it to give us information

46
00:02:26.566 --> 00:02:28.766
on a random position
that we want to generate,

47
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.900
versus our SpawnEnemyWave()
method is just doing something.

48
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:34.800
In this case,
doing the task

49
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.200
of creating different
numbers of enemies,

50
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.266
based on how many enemies we want.

51
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:42.333
In this case too, we need

52
00:02:42.466 --> 00:02:44.566
a Parameter
to tell SpawnEnemyWave()

53
00:02:44.566 --> 00:02:46.400
how many enemies we want to spawn,

54
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.333
but, in our GenerateSpawnPosition(),

55
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:51.800
we don't need any Parameters
because it's already doing

56
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:53.733
all the calculations
inside of the method.

57
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.600
So, what you're going to do is,

58
00:02:56.866 --> 00:02:59.100
in your SpawnEnemyWave() method,

59
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.633
you're going to add a Parameter,

60
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:05.866
which is an int of the number
of enemiesToSpawn.

61
00:03:06.633 --> 00:03:08.600
You're going to replace the 3

62
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:10.600
in i < 3 of your for() loop,

63
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.666
with the enemiesToSpawn variable,

64
00:03:13.666 --> 00:03:17.233
and then in your "Start()" method,
just make sure that in SpawnEnemyWave(),

65
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.933
you add an integer in there to tell

66
00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:23.666
the method how many enemies
you want to spawn. Now it's your turn.

67
00:03:24.433 --> 00:03:27.666
[music]
